        "title": "Music is the voice of the soul",
        "text": "It was the Greek philosopher Plato who said that music gave 'soul to the universe, wings to the mind, flight to the imagination, and charm and gaiety to life'. The Russian writer Leo Tolstoy called it the 'shorthand of emotion'. Since our earliest societies, we have filled our lives with music. Today we are surrounded more and more by music of every kind, which we sometimes cannot escape. What is different in our modern world is that, although we listen to more music, we make less of it.\n\nWhy does music affect us so powerfully? John Sloboda, a professor of psychology, believes there are three reasons. First, music is a strong source of personal associations. Research into the psychology of memory and of emotion suggests that at times of high emotion, your brain takes a 'recording' of everything else that was going on at that intense moment. So a piece of music linked to an emotional event in your life may well bring it flooding back when you hear it again. However, this reaction is unpredictable and, because it is individual, idiosyncratic.\n\nA second important reason lies in our physiology, which determines the range and organisation of the sounds we call music. Some of the most popular rhythmic patterns in music reflect rhythms in our own bodies, especially the heartbeat and breathing. This imitation extends to emotional signals, such as the way we use human speech: if you speak very slowly and let your voice fall at the end of words and sentences, anyone listening will assume you are rather depressed. In the same way, slow music with a falling cadence creates a feeling of sadness. Sloboda calls this an 'iconic connection': when you listen to music, you make links to innate human vocalisations of excitement, depression, anger and so on. This might explain the universal appeal of many forms of music, since basic human emotions are common to all cultures.\n\nFor Sloboda, the third reason is the most interesting. He points out that a key feature of our emotions is that they are tuned to detect change. The change may be positive (falling in love, winning the lottery) or negative (illness, bad luck), but either way its message is: pay attention now! In general, we humans recognise patterns easily and, more importantly, we also recognise departures from patterns. Since music is essentially pattern in sound, it is easy to see how it can 'hook' its listeners with subtle variations in melody, structure or rhythm. People pick up on the patterns and predict what will come next, without needing any formal training in music. When musical surprises happen, emotional responses are guaranteed.\n\nIn recent experiments, Sloboda has been plotting emotional highs and lows by asking his subjects to move a joystick while they listen to music. In this way he has been able to record a trace of their emotional reactions. Interestingly, unlike the personal associations, these reactions are not idiosyncratic: on the whole, people feel more or less emotion at the same points in the music. This is useful for science, because the researcher can isolate those points and ask what is going on.\n\nSo we may in fact have little control over the roller-coaster ride from sorrow to joy that music seems to take us on. Some melodies are quite manipulative and work on our emotions very effectively, and composers have often made full use of this. Take an orchestral piece by the Austrian composer Gustav Mahler, for example: for most listeners it is a gripping and totally involving experience. Such is the raw power of music, which should never be underestimated."
